        <p>The hemoglobin–oxygen dissociation curve is sigmoidal because of <strong>cooperative binding</strong>: each O₂ that binds makes the next bind more easily. A <strong>right shift</strong> means hemoglobin releases O₂ more readily, and is caused by exactly the conditions found in hard-working tissue — increased CO₂, increased H⁺ (lower pH, the <strong>Bohr effect</strong>), increased temperature, and increased 2,3-BPG. Fetal hemoglobin binds O₂ more tightly than adult hemoglobin (a left-shifted curve), letting it pull oxygen across the placenta.</p>
